.TH mbpy_grd3dplot 1 "12 September 2026" "MB-System 5.0" "MB-System 5.0"
.SH NAME
\fBmbpy_grd3dplot\fP \- Create an executable shellscript which will generate
a GMT modern-mode 3D perspective plot of gridded data.

.SH VERSION
Version 5.0

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmbpy_grd3dplot\fP \fB\-I\fP\fIgrid\fP [\fB\-O\fP\fIroot\fP]
[\fB\-\-format\fP\fIfmt\fP]
[\fB\-\-title\fP\fItext\fP]
[\fB\-\-colorbar\-label\fP\fItext\fP]
[\fB\-\-dpi\fP\fIdpi\fP]
[\fB\-X\fP] [\fB\-V\fP]
.br
[\fB\-R\fP\fIxmin/xmax/ymin/ymax\fP]
[\fB\-J\fP\fIspec\fP]
[\fB\-P\fP\fIpagesize\fP]
[\fB\-\-portrait\fP | \fB\-\-landscape\fP]
[\fB\-\-scale\-location\fP\fI{l,r,t,b}\fP]
[\fB\-\-tick\-info\fP\fIspec\fP]
[\fB\-\-length\-scale\fP\fIspec\fP]
.br
[\fB\-E\fP\fIazimuth/elevation\fP]
[\fB\-F\fP\fIexaggeration\fP]
[\fB\-\-zscale\fP\fIval\fP]
.br
[\fB\-\-color\-mode\fP\fI{1-7}\fP]
[\fB\-W\fP\fIspec\fP]
[\fB\-D\fP]
[\fB\-Y\fP]
[\fB\-\-stretch\-color\fP]
[\fB\-Z\fP\fIzmin/zmax\fP]
[\fB\-\-data\-scale\fP\fIfactor\fP]
.br
[\fB\-\-magnitude\fP\fIval\fP]
[\fB\-\-azimuth\fP\fIdeg\fP]
[\fB\-\-elevation\fP\fIdeg\fP]
[\fB\-\-shade\-flip\fP]
[\fB\-\-intensity\-file\fP\fIgrid\fP]
[\fB\-\-stretch\-shade\fP]
.br
[\fB\-\-mesh\-contour\-pen\fP\fIpen\fP]
[\fB\-\-drape\-file\fP\fIgrid\fP]
[\fB\-\-mesh\-null\fP[\fIlevel/r/g/b\fP]]
[\fB\-\-mesh\-zlevel\fP\fIval\fP]
.br
[\fB\-\-coast\fP]
[\fB\-\-coast\-resolution\fP\fI{f,h,i,l,c}\fP]
[\fB\-\-coast\-dry\-fill\fP\fIcolor\fP]
[\fB\-\-coast\-wet\-fill\fP\fIcolor\fP]
[\fB\-\-coast\-lake\-fill\fP\fIcolor\fP]
[\fB\-\-coast\-river\fP\fIcolor\fP]
[\fB\-\-coast\-boundary\fP\fIspec\fP]
[\fB\-\-coast\-pen\fP\fIpen\fP]
.br
[\fB\-\-text\fP\fIlon/lat/size/angle/font/justify/text\fP]
[\fB\-\-gmt\-default\fP\fIparam/value\fP]

.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBmbpy_grd3dplot\fP is a Python/PyGMT-era replacement for the Perl
macro \fBmbm_grd3dplot\fP. It reads a grid and generates an executable
shellscript of \fBGMT\fP \fBmodern mode\fP commands, built around
\fBgmt grdview\fP, which produces a 3D perspective view of that grid
when run. Several styles of view can be generated: color fill,
color fill shaded by synthetic illumination, color fill shaded by a
supplied intensity grid, color fill of slope magnitude, color fill
shaded by slope magnitude, a plain wireframe mesh, and a wireframe mesh
with contour lines.

\fBmbpy_grd3dplot\fP shares its page-layout, projection-fitting, and
color-palette conventions with \fBmbpy_grdplot\fP (and, in fact, its
underlying Python modules \fBmbpy_layout.py\fP/\fBmbpy_color.py\fP/
\fBmbpy_misc.py\fP), so a plot's color scheme and overall sizing behave
the same way across both tools. What is specific to a 3D view is the
viewpoint (\fB\-\-view\fP, azimuth/elevation of the observer) and the
vertical exaggeration/z-scale (\fB\-\-exaggeration\fP or
\fB\-\-zscale\fP): the effective footprint of the plot on the page is
corrected for the chosen viewing azimuth, and only two of the four
possible vertical frame walls (whichever face the viewer) are
annotated.

Unlike \fBmbm_grd3dplot\fP's single-letter \fB\-M\fP\fIsub\fP\fB:\fP\fIsub\fP\fB:\fP...
grammar, \fBmbpy_grd3dplot\fP exposes each concept as its own
descriptive long option (see OPTIONS below).

\fBmbpy_grd3dplot\fP does not expose a \fB\-\-contour\fP option:
\fBmbm_grd3dplot\fP itself defines a \fB\-C\fP\fIcontour_control\fP
option, but that value is never actually passed to any GMT plotting
command in the original macro -- it is a no-op. The one real contour
capability \fBmbm_grd3dplot\fP has is drawing contour lines directly on
a mesh plot via \fB\-\-color\-mode\fP 7's \fB\-\-mesh\-contour\-pen\fP.

\fBNote:\fP on at least GMT 6.7.0, \fB\-\-color\-mode\fP 7 (mesh with
contour lines) can trigger a segmentation fault inside \fBgmt grdview\fP
itself when a 3D perspective and \fB\-Jz\fP vertical scaling are both
active together with a contour pen (\fB\-Qm \-W\fP\fIpen\fP) -- this
reproduces regardless of the pen syntax used, and appears to be an
upstream GMT bug rather than anything \fBmbpy_grd3dplot\fP can control
or work around. Plain mesh plots (\fB\-\-color\-mode\fP 6, no contour
pen) are unaffected.

\fBmbpy_grd3dplot\fP requires \fBGMT\fP 6 or later (modern mode) and a
Python 3 interpreter. It depends on the same sibling support modules as
\fBmbpy_grdplot\fP (\fBmbpy_layout.py\fP, \fBmbpy_color.py\fP,
\fBmbpy_misc.py\fP) plus its own \fBmbpy_grd3dplot_script.py\fP,
installed alongside it.

